WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.813 --> 00:00:02.136
- Sheep, sheep, sheep!

00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:03.048
Hi, guys.

00:00:03.048 --> 00:00:03.883
Hi, Bubbles.

00:00:03.883 --> 00:00:04.864
Hi, Walter.

00:00:04.864 --> 00:00:05.715
Hi, Dolly.

00:00:05.715 --> 00:00:06.548
Hey, momma.

00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:07.381
Hi, baby.

00:00:07.381 --> 00:00:08.280
Hi, Alvie.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.014
(sheep bellowing)

00:00:11.014 --> 00:00:13.139
- One, two, three.

00:00:13.139 --> 00:00:15.044
- Janie's farm didn't
start out with animals

00:00:15.044 --> 00:00:16.710
that were missing limbs.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.410
It just happened to come to her farm

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.644
and she felt, well, prothetic
technology's helped me,

00:00:22.644 --> 00:00:25.061
it could certainly help them.

00:00:26.977 --> 00:00:28.499
(rooster crowing)

00:00:28.499 --> 00:00:29.921
(celebratory music)

00:00:29.921 --> 00:00:31.745
- I can't imagine not having
not having an artificial leg

00:00:31.745 --> 00:00:35.512
because I lost my leg at 10
and I'm quite used to it.

00:00:35.512 --> 00:00:38.687
It's thanks to medical
science that I'm able to

00:00:38.687 --> 00:00:40.358
live this normal life.

00:00:40.358 --> 00:00:41.465
And for these animals,

00:00:41.465 --> 00:00:43.026
I want no different for them.

00:00:43.026 --> 00:00:44.058
Hi, Felix.

00:00:44.058 --> 00:00:47.607
What goes on at Woodstock Farm
Sanctuary on a daily basis

00:00:47.607 --> 00:00:51.337
we're home to right now around
300 rescued farm animals.

00:00:51.337 --> 00:00:53.131
They've all come from bad places

00:00:53.131 --> 00:00:56.105
and they get the veterinary care,

00:00:56.105 --> 00:00:58.761
the healthcare that they
need on a daily basis.

00:00:58.761 --> 00:00:59.594
(cow mooing)

00:00:59.594 --> 00:01:00.427
- [Man] Hi, Precious.

00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:02.207
(laughing)

00:01:02.207 --> 00:01:03.040
- [Man] What's the matter with you?

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.961
- [Man] No, she's feisty.

00:01:04.961 --> 00:01:06.772
- Can you come help me with Felix?

00:01:06.772 --> 00:01:10.694
They're all actually in the front pasture.

00:01:10.694 --> 00:01:14.182
Little Felix came to us a baby lamb

00:01:14.182 --> 00:01:16.638
and he had come from a big sheep farm.

00:01:16.638 --> 00:01:19.360
And overnight, some sort
of predator had gotten in

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.941
and they found little Felix with

00:01:21.941 --> 00:01:23.956
his leg chewed off.

00:01:23.956 --> 00:01:26.376
And so, I spoke to my prosthetist,

00:01:26.376 --> 00:01:29.415
the gentleman who makes my legs, Eric.

00:01:29.415 --> 00:01:31.427
- Ah, I don't if many clinicians

00:01:31.427 --> 00:01:33.294
would have went out to the farm.

00:01:33.294 --> 00:01:34.268
I don't have any experience

00:01:34.268 --> 00:01:36.253
with fitting prosthetics on animals.

00:01:36.253 --> 00:01:38.332
Very few people in the country do.

00:01:38.332 --> 00:01:41.393
So, I figured if I could take
what knowledge that I do have

00:01:41.393 --> 00:01:42.758
and apply it to the animal

00:01:42.758 --> 00:01:47.430
I could probably get them
to utilize a prosthesis.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.265
- And it wasn't until we
got hooked up SUNY New Paltz

00:01:51.265 --> 00:01:53.402
and their 3-D printing lab

00:01:53.402 --> 00:01:55.882
that we were able to make
something pretty cool

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:57.965
and cutting edge for him.

00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:03.171
- Seeing him walk on the
leg was absolutely amazing.

00:02:03.171 --> 00:02:04.578
From my limited interactions with him,

00:02:04.578 --> 00:02:07.765
he isn't necessarily the most
outgoing sheep in the world.

00:02:07.765 --> 00:02:09.459
He's actually very sheepish.

00:02:09.459 --> 00:02:11.274
But, what he ended up doing was

00:02:11.274 --> 00:02:14.772
he started running away
from us which was amazing.

00:02:14.772 --> 00:02:16.852
- We were just jumping for
joy that he could actually

00:02:16.852 --> 00:02:18.122
get up and run,

00:02:18.122 --> 00:02:20.360
keep up with his flock.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.506
He looked like he was a very happy sheep.

00:02:22.506 --> 00:02:25.425
If we could do this more on other animals

00:02:25.425 --> 00:02:27.437
we wouldn’t, you know, have to put them down.

00:02:27.437 --> 00:02:30.222
- Most of the world doesn't
fully understand it.

00:02:30.222 --> 00:02:32.186
They think of them as just farmed animals,

00:02:32.186 --> 00:02:34.783
but we hope that that changes.

00:02:34.783 --> 00:02:36.993
And that's part of the
magic of what we do here.

00:02:36.993 --> 00:02:39.469
They get pastures to roam in,

00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:42.793
access to the sun and to the grass.

00:02:42.793 --> 00:02:46.183
They live life the way
they were intended to live.

00:02:46.183 --> 00:02:48.100
There's no greater joy.

